{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center enables account access and application use in multiple AWS Regions - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center is a service that helps manage access to AWS accounts and applications for workforces. It is now possible to replicate IAM Identity Center from the primary AWS Region to additional Regions of choice, enhancing user access resilience and enabling deployment of AWS applications in preferred Regions. This feature automatically replicates identities, entitlements, and other information from the primary Region to additional Regions. IAM Identity Center users can continue to access their AWS accounts using the already provisioned entitlements in the additional Regions during disruptions in the primary Region. AWS application administrators can deploy applications in additional Regions using the standard application deployment workflow and assign users to the application in that Region. IAM Identity Center multi-Region support is available in 17 commercial AWS Regions for organization instances connected to an external identity provider, such as Okta, and requires a multi-Region customer managed KMS key. Standard AWS KMS charges apply for storing and using CMKs. IAM Identity Center is provided at no additional cost. To learn more and get started, visit the IAM Identity Center product detail page and user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/aws-iam-identity-center-multi-region-aws-account-access-and-application-deployment/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"deploying-custom-domain-rest-api","skill_description":"Deploys a Regional REST API with a custom domain name, a Lambda backend function, and a request-based Lambda authorizer using AWS CLI. Covers ACM certificate provisioning, API Gateway REST API creation, Lambda function deployment, request authorizer setup, custom domain configuration, base path mapping, and Route 53 DNS record creation. Trigger keywords: custom domain, REST API, Lambda, Route 53, API Gateway, regional endpoint, request authorizer, base path mapping.\n\n\nServices: api-gateway, lambda, route-53, acm\nTasks: deploy, configure\nPersona: backend-developer\nWorkload: serverless, networking\n*","skill_name":"deploying-custom-domain-rest-api"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"arc-region-switch","skill_description":"Answers questions about Amazon Application Recovery Controller (ARC) Region switch including architecture, plans, execution blocks, workflows, triggers, active/active vs active/passive, cross-account support, recovery time, dashboards, and customer positioning. Applicable when users ask about ARC Region switch adoption, design, or troubleshooting.\n\nServices: arc-region-switch\nTasks: learn, architect, build, debug, migrate, advise\nPersona: developer, devops, solutions-architect\nWorkload: disaster-recovery, multi-region\n*","skill_name":"arc-region-switch"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether Q Business requires same-region IAM Identity Center instance", "parent_id": "2f64ce2e-02d9-4b93-8210-0bb0ee47ddc4"}

