{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"IAM Identity Center now enables programmatic AWS account access for customer managed applications - AWS","context":"IAM Identity Center, a service offered by AWS, has introduced a new feature that enables customer managed applications to programmatically access AWS accounts on behalf of their users. This is achieved by configuring the external identity provider (IdP) as a trusted token issuer (TTI) in IAM Identity Center. Once configured, users who have already signed in through the IdP can access their assigned AWS accounts and obtain temporary security credentials for their authorized roles without undergoing a separate authentication flow. This eliminates redundant sign-ins. This feature is available for organization instances of IAM Identity Center and can be enabled by management account administrators or delegated administrators. It is available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the China Regions. To enable this feature, navigate to the IAM Identity Center console, select the customer managed application, and enable AWS account access. For more information, refer to the IAM Identity Center User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/aws-iam-identity-center-account-access-customer-managed-apps/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center makes managment of AWS account access optional for new organization instances - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center now allows users to opt out of managing AWS account access when creating a new organization instance. This feature enables IAM Identity Center to manage access to AWS applications without the need to manage access to AWS accounts. Previously, managing access to AWS accounts was a requirement for using IAM Identity Center. This optional feature can be enabled during the initial configuration of an IAM Identity Center instance and does not affect existing instances. When account management is disabled, IAM Identity Center does not provision its service-linked role into member accounts, reducing the access surface in the environment. Account management permissions can be enabled later through instance settings or the UpdateInstance API. This capability is available in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available. To get started, users can refer to the Configure instance settings section in the IAM Identity Center User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/aws-identity-center-accounts-optional/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS IAM Identity Center supports one-click multi-Region option for new organization instances - AWS","context":"AWS IAM Identity Center has introduced a one-click option for enabling multi-Region support when creating a new organization instance. Previously, this process involved several steps such as creating a customer managed KMS key, configuring key policies, and manually adding Regions. Now, customers can choose from three instance configuration options: single-Region instance, multi-Region instance, or custom instance. The multi-Region instance option automatically creates a customer managed multi-Region KMS key and replicates the instance to an additional Region, ensuring resilient AWS account and application access. This feature is available in 17 commercial AWS Regions for organization instances of IAM Identity Center, with standard AWS KMS charges applying for the customer managed key. To get started, refer to the IAM Identity Center User Guide and the Using IAM Identity Center across multiple AWS Regions page. IAM Identity Center remains free of charge","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/aws-iam-identity-center-supports-one-click-multi-region-option-new-organization-instances/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether IAM Identity Center ListInstances API works from member accounts for org-level instance", "parent_id": "2f64ce2e-02d9-4b93-8210-0bb0ee47ddc4"}
